  "reasoning": "The claim that \"lightning never strikes the same place twice\" is a well-documented myth. According to the National Weather Service (NWS) and atmospheric scientists, lightning often strikes the same location repeatedly, particularly tall, pointed, or isolated structures that offer the path of least resistance to ground. For instance, the Empire State Building in New York City is struck by lightning an average of 20 to 25 times every year.",
